/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A magnetostrictive material changes shape when a magnetic field passes through it.
   - λ is the magnetostriction as a dimensionless fraction; multiply by 10⁶ to express it in ppm. ΔL and L are in the same unit.
   - The inverse is the Villari effect: stress applied to the material changes its magnetization.
   - The curve of λ against field is not a straight line.
   - Nickel gives small negative magnetostriction and is cheap and stable.
   - A magneto-elastic torque sensor uses a shaft with a magnetostrictive band or ring.
   - A magnetostrictive delay line sends a current pulse through a wire or ribbon.
   - The most common industrial use is the magnetostrictive position sensor.
   - x is the distance of the magnet from the pickup in metres; v is the speed of the torsional strain wave along the waveguide in m/s; t is the time from the current pulse to the detected return in seconds.
   - A magnetostrictive ultrasonic transducer uses a coil around a nickel or Terfenol-D rod.
*/
