/* book Worked example 2: Q: A magnetostrictive position sensor on a hydraulic cylinder has a waveguide wave speed of 2800 m/s. The return pulse arrives 250 µs after the current pulse. (a) Find the magnet position. (b) The timer counts a 100 MHz clock. Find the position resolution.
   1. Data: v = 2800 m/s, t = 250 µs = 250 × 10⁻⁶ s, clock 100 MHz, so one clock period is 1 / 10⁸ = 10 ns.
   2. (a) x = v t = 2800 × 250 × 10⁻⁶ = 0.70 m.
   3. (b) One clock count is 10 ns, so the smallest step of distance is v × 10 ns = 2800 × 10 × 10⁻⁹ = 2.8 × 10⁻⁵ m = 28 µm.
   4. Check: 0.70 m / 28 µm = 25 000 counts, which matches 250 µs / 10 ns = 25 000 clock periods.
   Answer: The magnet is 0.70 m from the head, and one clock count is 28 µm of travel. */
