/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Photoelectric transducers use the photoelectric effect: light of frequency f delivers photons of energy E = hf.
   - Photoelectric transducers come in three classes.
   - The beam can be arranged in three common ways.
   - A fibre has a core of refractive index n1 surrounded by cladding of refractive index n2, with n1 > n2.
   - NA is the numerical aperture, a dimensionless number; n1 is the core refractive index; n2 is the cladding refractive index.
   - θa is the acceptance angle, the largest half-angle at the input for which light is guided; NA is the numerical aperture.
   - An intensity-modulated fibre sensor makes the sensor head change how much light reaches the photodiode.
   - Pin is input optical power in mW or W; Pout is output optical power in the same unit; Loss(dB) is positive when Pout < Pin.
   - A fibre Bragg grating is a periodic change in the core refractive index.
   - In medicine, a pulse oximeter clipped on a finger is a through-beam photoelectric sensor: red and infrared LEDs on one side, a photodiode on the other.
*/
